 ### [Bricking](914-bricking.md)
-Bricking is a memory management technique for volume rendering that partitions a volumetric dataset larger than the available GPU memory (or texture unit limits) into smaller, fixed-size sub-volumes called "bricks". Each brick is loaded into GPU memory, rendered independently, and the resulting images are composited to form the final image. It differs from [Chunk-based Voxel Data Structure](1818-chunk-based-voxel-data-structure.md) by typically using uniform, fixed-size blocks optimized for texture sampling, and from [Tile-based Volume Ray Marching](1678-tile-based-volume-ray-marching.md) by operating on 3D spatial subdivisions rather than 2D screen tiles.
+Bricking is a memory management technique that subdivides volumetric datasets larger than the available GPU texture memory into smaller, equally-sized sub-volumes called bricks, allowing them to be loaded on-demand and rendered individually. It solves the hardware limitation where a single 3D texture cannot hold the entire volume, enabling out-of-core visualization by treating the GPU memory as a cache for these bricks. Unlike [Brick-based Volume Raycasting](3341-brick-based-volume-raycasting.md), which focuses on the ray traversal logic, or [Dynamic Rebricking](2823-dynamic-rebricking.md), which decouples physical and logical sizes, standard Bricking uses fixed-size bricks for both storage and rendering, relying on overlap and sorting to maintain visual continuity.
 
