+### Homogeneous Brick Optimization
+- [S2, unofficial] Nodes corresponding to homogeneous bricks (all voxels contain the same value up to a user-defined threshold) store the average value in the brick instead of a pointer to the brick's location in the brick cache.
+- [S2, unofficial] For multi-channel data, a node can only be marked homogeneous if its corresponding bricks in all channels are homogeneous.
 
 ## Background
-Bricking was introduced to overcome hardware limitations regarding texture memory size and resolution limits (e.g., $512^3$ textures or 256MB video memory on older GPUs like the NVIDIA GeForce 6) [S1, unofficial]. Early implementations used an oldest-out-first swapping strategy for supervolumes that did not fit in GPU memory [S2, unofficial]. The technique evolved to include multi-resolution hierarchies and LOD-based caching to handle increasingly large datasets in interactive applications [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial]. It is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time rendering engines where data exceeds available VRAM [S1, S2, S3, S4, unofficial].
+Bricking was introduced as a solution to the limited texture memory of early GPUs (e.g., NVIDIA GeForce 6 with $512^3$ resolution limits and 256MB memory caps) [S1, unofficial]. It enabled the visualization of volumes far exceeding hardware constraints by treating GPU memory as a cache for on-demand loaded bricks [S2, unofficial][S6, unofficial]. The technique has since evolved to include multi-resolution hierarchies [S1, unofficial], specialized caching policies like LRU [S2, unofficial], and integration with compression schemes for segmentation volumes [S1, unofficial]. It is widely used in scientific visualization, medical imaging, and real-time rendering engines where large datasets must be explored interactively.
 
